meta {
  name: D1 - Touch Issue Updated At
  type: graphql
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}
  body: graphql
  auth: inherit
}

body:graphql {
  mutation TouchIssue($issueId: String!) {
    issueUpdate(id: $issueId, input: { description: "Welcome to Linear! \n\n(touched by source-linear verification)" }) {
      success
      issue { id identifier title createdAt updatedAt archivedAt }
    }
  }
}

body:graphql:vars {
  {
    "issueId": "4937bc7e-6bda-4612-9a80-3fbaaec872d9"
  }
}

docs {
  Reference: https://linear.app/developers/graphql

  **Only the user runs this. Never the assistant.** This is a state-mutating call.

  Bumps the `updatedAt` of exactly ONE issue (`EST-1`, "Get familiar with Linear") so the
  workspace stops being a degenerate all-timestamps-tied dataset. Targets `description`,
  a no-op-shaped field that no other stream's snapshot depends on.

  Closes TWO gaps that are currently the biggest holes in this cluster's verification:

  1. **Ascending `updatedAt` order is unproven.** `11 - Issues Ascending Sweep` showed the
     `sort` mechanism works (via `title`), but with every `updatedAt` identical it could
     not show that `sort: [{updatedAt: {order: Ascending}}]` actually orders by that key.
     The connector's per-page checkpointing for Issues / Projects / Initiatives rests
     entirely on this.
  2. **The `gt` cursor has never been observed NARROWING a non-empty result.** Every
     filter probe so far returns either all rows or zero rows, because all rows share one
     timestamp. After this mutation, `filter: {updatedAt: {gt: "<the other 3 issues'
     timestamp>"}}` should return EXACTLY this one issue — the first true partial narrowing
     in the collection.

  **Re-run after:** `11 - Issues Ascending Sweep` (confirm the touched issue now sorts LAST
  under `Ascending`, i.e. the highest `updatedAt` is at the end) and
  `07 - Cursor Filter Boundary` (confirm `gt: 2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z` now returns 1 row
  instead of 0).

  Please report the touched issue's new `updatedAt` so the follow-up filters can be pinned.
}
